  "source_path": "/scratch/ziv_baretto/Thesis_Ziv/Capstone-Thesis-/DATA_SET_BUILDER_AND_EXPLORER/Timeline_Maker/output_merged_v3/food_safety/Pradeep vs State on 12 July, 2017.json",
  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Pradeep vs State on 12 July, 2017",
    "facts": "The petitioner has preferred this criminal misc. petition under Section 482 Cr.P.C. against the order dated 22.02.2017 passed by learned Sessions Judge, Jodhpur in criminal revision No.52/2016 by which he while dismissing the revision petition filed by the petitioner has upheld the order dated 02.07.2016 passed by learned Chief Judicial Magistrate, Jodhpur in criminal case No.56/2014(646/2011) by which he framed charges against the petitioner for the offence under Section 7, (I)/16 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, and to quash and [LEAKAGE_MASK] the same. 2. The brief facts as noticed by this Court are that the sample was drawn by the respondent on 23.03.2011 and was sent for analysis on 24.03.2011. The report of the analysis was received on 17.05.2011 and upon such report, a complaint was filed on 21.07.2011. The report from the said laboratory disclosed (2 of 7) [CRLMP-1376/2017] that the standards laid down in the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 (hereinafter referred to as Act of 1954) and Rules were made and thus, pre-charge evidence of the Food Inspector were recorded and the charges were framed against the petitioner on 19.09.2015. The petitioner challenged the framing of charges by filing a revision petition whereby the matter was remanded back for fresh consideration. The charges were against framed on 02.07.2017 on fresh consideration and revision was filed against the same but the order was upheld vide order dated 22.02.2017.",
    "arguments": "3. Learned counsel for the petitioner has submitted that the rights granted to the petitioner under Section 13(2) of the Act of 1954 have been infringed and therefore, since his valuable rights have been infringed, the trial cannot go on. The issues raised by the petitioner has been dealt by both the courts below but they have not concluded impact of liberty to the petitioner in view of Section 13(2) Pradeep vs State on 12 July, 2017 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/152257299/ 1 of the Act of 1954. 4. Learned counsel for the petitioner has relied upon the judgment passed by a coordinate Bench of this Court in Vikram Arora & Ors. Vs. State & Anr. in S.B. Criminal Misc. Petition No.1988/2016 decided on 05.09.2016. Supreme Court, in Municipal Corporation of Delhi Vs. Ghisa Ram (AIR 1967 SC 970), while examining scope of sub-sec.(2) and (5) of Section 13 of the Act, concluded in clear and unequivocal term that right of accused to get sample examined by the Director of Central Food Laboratory is a valuable right and if this right is infringed, his conviction cannot be sustained. The Court held: \"It appears to us that when a valuable right is conferred by S. 13 (2) of the Act on the vendor to have the sample given to him analysed by the Director of the Central Food Laboratory, it is to be expected that the prosecution will proceed in such a manner that that right will not be denied to him. The same principle is also reiterated in a latter judgment in case of Girishbhai Dahyabhai Shah Vs. C.C. Jani Anr. [(2009) 15 SCC 64] wherein the Court held: Pradeep vs State on 12 July, 2017 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/152257299/ 2 (5 of 7) [CRLMP-1376/2017] A Coordinate Bench of this Court at Jaipur, in Nemi Chand Agrawal Vs. State of Rajasthan [2012(2) Cr.L.R. (Raj.) 830], noticing violation of Section 13(2) of the Act has also [LEAKAGE_MASK] the complaint under Section 7/16 of the Act.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "3. Learned counsel for the petitioner has submitted that the rights granted to the petitioner under Section 13(2) of the Act of 1954 have been infringed and therefore, since his valuable rights have been infringed, the trial cannot go on. The issues raised by the petitioner has been dealt by both the courts below but they have not concluded impact of liberty to the petitioner in view of Section 13(2) Pradeep vs State on 12 July, 2017 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/152257299/ 1 of the Act of 1954. 4. Learned counsel for the petitioner has relied upon the judgment passed by a coordinate Bench of this Court in Vikram Arora & Ors. Vs. State & Anr. in S.B. Criminal Misc. Petition No.1988/2016 decided on 05.09.2016.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "Supreme Court, in Municipal Corporation of Delhi Vs. Ghisa Ram (AIR 1967 SC 970), while examining scope of sub-sec.(2) and (5) of Section 13 of the Act, concluded in clear and unequivocal term that right of accused to get sample examined by the Director of Central Food Laboratory is a valuable right and if this right is infringed, his conviction cannot be sustained. The Court held: \"It appears to us that when a valuable right is conferred by S. 13 (2) of the Act on the vendor to have the sample given to him analysed by the Director of the Central Food Laboratory, it is to be expected that the prosecution will proceed in such a manner that that right will not be denied to him. The same principle is also reiterated in a latter judgment in case of Girishbhai Dahyabhai Shah Vs. C.C. Jani Anr. [(2009) 15 SCC 64] wherein the Court held: Pradeep vs State on 12 July, 2017 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/152257299/ 2 (5 of 7) [CRLMP-1376/2017] A Coordinate Bench of this Court at Jaipur, in Nemi Chand Agrawal Vs. State of Rajasthan [2012(2) Cr.L.R. (Raj.) 830], noticing violation of Section 13(2) of the Act has also [LEAKAGE_MASK] the complaint under Section 7/16 of the Act."
  },
